      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the prevalence of positive results for methacholine challenge tests in asymptomatic Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) cadets with no history of asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective, blinded cohort comparison study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Pulmonary diseases clinic in a US Army tertiary-care medical center."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "One hundred three college students who were undergoing a physical examination before entering active duty. Group 1 subjects, 58 men and 5 women with an average age of 22.7 years, had no symptoms or personal history of asthma. Group 2 patients, 34 men and 6 women with an average age of 22.2 years, had a history or recent suggestive symptoms of asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Methacholine challenge testing using concentrations of 0.025, 0.25, 2.5, 10, and 25 mg/mL for a total dose of 188 inhalation units or until FEV(1) had declined by 20%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Group 2 had significantly more patients with positive results for methacholine challenge tests or reversible airflow obstruction at baseline (23 of 40 patients [57.5%]) than group 1 (8 of 63 patients [12.7%]; p < 0.05). The cadets in group 1 with positive results for methacholine challenge tests reacted with a 20% decline in FEV(1) at the following concentrations: 25 mg/mL (188 IU), 2 patients; 10 mg/mL (64 IU), 4 patients; and 2.5 mg/mL (13.8 IU), 2 patients. Using values calculated for the provocative concentration of a substance causing a 20% fall in FEV(1) and the new American Thoracic Society criteria, four patients would have borderline bronchial hyperresponsiveness (4 to 16 mg/mL) and three patients (4.8%) would have mild bronchial hyperresponsiveness (1 to 4 mg/mL)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Asymptomatic US Army ROTC cadets with no history of asthma have possible false-positive responses to methacholine at concentrations > 0.25 mg/mL."
        }
      ]},
